== Taxonomy ==
Despite its scientific name, alsike clover is not of [[hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] origin. The plant gets its common name from the town of [[Alsike, Sweden|Alsike]] in [[Sweden]] from which [[Carl Linnaeus]] first described it. He thought it was a cross between [[Trifolium repens|''T.&nbsp;repens'']] (white clover) and [[Trifolium pratense|''T.&nbsp;pratense'']] (red clover), but in this he was mistaken and it is a separate species.<ref>[http://www.animalrangeextension.montana.edu/articles/forage/species/legumes/alsikeclover.htm Publications & Information: Alsike Clover] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121230123657/http://animalrangeextension.montana.edu/articles/forage/Species/Legumes/Alsikeclover.htm |date=2012-12-30 }}. Montana State University.</ref><ref>Clark, G. H. (1913). [http://chestofbooks.com/flora-plants/weeds/Fodder-Pasture-Plants/Alsike-Clover-Trifolium-Hybridum-L.html Fodder and Pasture Plants.] Canada Dept. of Agriculture.</ref>

== Distribution and habitat ==
Originating in mainland Europe, it has become established as an introduced plant in the [[British Isles]]<ref>[[C. A. Stace]], ''Interactive Flora of the British Isles, a Digital Encyclopaedia'': ''Trifolium hybridum''. {{ISBN|90-75000-69-3}}. ([http://nlbif.eti.uva.nl/bis/flora.php?selected=beschrijving&menuentry=soorten&id=2960 Online version)] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110608014308/http://nlbif.eti.uva.nl/bis/flora.php?selected=beschrijving&menuentry=soorten&id=2960 |date=2011-06-08 }}</ref> and throughout the [[temperateness|temperate]] regions of the world.<ref>A R Clapham, T G Tutin, E F Warburg, ''Flora of the British Isles'', Cambridge, 1962, p 341</ref> It is native to southern Europe and southwestern Asia, especially in mountainous regions.{{Citation needed|date=April 2026}}

Because of its wide cultivation and use as [[forage]], its [[subspecies]] ''T.&nbsp;h. hybridum'' is used and has become naturalised further north in Europe and in other parts of the world. Its natural habitat is fields, meadows, roadsides, banks and waste ground. When added to seed mixtures, it seldom persists once the sward has closed up.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.brc.ac.uk/plantatlas/index.php?q=node/2190 |title=''Trifolium hybridum'' (Alsike Clover) |work=Online Atlas of the British and Irish Flora |accessdate=2013-12-24}}</ref>

== Toxicity ==
There is limited evidence of ''T. hybridum'' having toxic effects on horses including [[Photosensitivity|photosensitization]] and Alsike clover poisoning. Photosensitivity in horses, also known as alsike rash, is a reversible condition in which the mechanism is unknown.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=Nation |first=P. Nicholas |date=May 1989 |journal=Can Vet J |volume=30 |issue=5 |pages=410–5 |title=Alsike clover poisoning: A review |pmid=17423321 |pmc=1681272}}</ref> A diet in equestrians of at least 20% ''T.&nbsp;hybridum'' over the course of four weeks could lead to poisoning which includes deleterious neurological effects and liver failure. The active toxin is unknown.<ref name=":4">{{cite book |chapter=Alsike Clover ( ''Trifolium hybridum'' ) |date=19 September 2021 |title=Blackwell's Five‐Minute Veterinary Consult Clinical Companion |pages=259–264 |editor-last=Hovda |editor-first=Lynn R. |edition=1st |publisher=Wiley |language=en |doi=10.1002/9781119671527.ch51 |isbn=978-1-119-67149-7 |editor2-last=Benson |editor2-first=Dionne |editor3-last=Poppenga |editor3-first=Robert H.}}</ref>

The plant can irritate skin if handled.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Reiner |first=Ralph E. |title=Introducing the Flowering Beauty of Glacier National Park and the Majestic High Rockies |publisher=Glacier Park, Inc. |year=1969 |pages=122}}</ref>

== Uses ==
The species has been found to quickly lower [[soil pH]], increasing its viable area of reproduction,<ref name=":22">Paplauskienė, V., & Dabkevičienė, G. (2012). [http://zemdirbyste-agriculture.lt/99(3)tomas/99_3_tomas_str12.pdf A study of genetic diversity in Trifolium hybridum varieties using morphological characters and ISSR markers]. ''Žemdirbystė–Agriculture'', ''99'', 313-318.</ref> whilst reducing the bio-density of weeds by up to 57%.<ref name=":022">{{Cite journal |last=Ross |first=Shirley M. |last2=King |first2=Jane R. |last3=Izaurralde |first3=R.César |last4=O'Donovan |first4=John T. |date=July 2001 |title=Weed Suppression by Seven Clover Species |journal=Agronomy Journal |language=en |volume=93 |issue=4 |pages=820–827 |doi=10.2134/agronj2001.934820x |issn=0002-1962}}</ref> Under the right conditions it may become invasive unless properly managed.<ref name=":32">{{cite web |title=ALSIKE CLOVER |work=Plant Fact Sheet/Guide |publisher=National Plant Data Center |url=https://www.nrcs.usda.gov/plantmaterials/idpmcpg8306.pdf}}</ref> With regard to inbreeding, it was found that there were disadvantageous changes to height, persistence, and vigor unless selectively bred for said traits.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Townsend |first=C. E. |last2=Remmenga |first2=E. E. |date=March–April 1968 |title=Inbreeding in Tetraploid Alsike Clover, ''Trifolium bybridum'' L. |journal=Crop Science |language=en |volume=8 |issue=2 |pages=213–217 |doi=10.2135/cropsci1968.0011183X000800020024x |issn=0011-183X}}</ref>

It is also grown as [[fodder]] ([[hay]] or [[silage]]).{{Citation needed|date=April 2026}}

